WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
STUDENT: Sure. My problem concerns what you said about democracy 

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
not being so good handling the chronic problems--

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
SHAPIRO: Yeah.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.000
STUDENT: --and I'm wondering if you can offer what would be solutions to a chronic problem

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
and if globalization can help or hinder,

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.920
and if religion comes to play at all in that. 

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.810
SHAPIR0: Ok. So let me set the religion piece aside first and address the other parts. 

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:45.590
I think that in the kind of political theorizing that interests me, the question

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.810
is always, "Compared to what?" 

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:55.710
So, democracy's not very good at handling chronic problems, but compared to what? 

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.679
You know,

00:00:56.679 --> 00:00:59.820
we might have thought that

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:06.000
--the boatloads of literature that's come out of the analytical

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.600
tradition of political theory,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.910
which tells you why democracies are not good

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.050
at handling

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.250
all kinds of

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.210
chronic problems, like

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.210
tragedy of the commons problems,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.210
public goods provision,

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.390
and so on.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:28.240
But you know, one of the ironies of the collapse of the Soviet Union is that we

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.540
discovered

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:35.210
they didn't handle these things well at all, you
know? The lakes in central Russia

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.130
are toxic pits. They didn't handle environmental problems well either. 

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.980
So democracy is not very good at it, but compared to what? And I think that the

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.329
answer seems to be:

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:51.619
certainly not  compared to authoritarian systems. So the interesting question then 

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:52.920
becomes:

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.009
which types of democracies are better and worse at it?

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:59.060
And I think that--

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.240
we created a democracy here

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.800
that's not particularly good at it.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.869
Because we created a democracy here

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:14.130
which doesn't allow governments to act. We built in what political economists call

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.459
"veto points" all over the place,

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:18.929
right? We

00:02:18.929 --> 00:02:23.789
We created bicameralism, we created separation of powers, we created a federal system, 

00:02:23.789 --> 00:02:25.250
we overrepresented

00:02:26.169 --> 00:02:31.599
we overrepresented the small states in the Senate. We've got-- as we all can watch playing

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:32.410
out now--

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:39.410
the filibuster rule. All of this stuff makes innovation-- political innovation-- really hard, ok?

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.340
And it's,

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.749
in an important sense,

00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:45.150
all of it is anti-democratic,

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.180
because one important analogue--

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.170
one important reason 

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.309
that

00:02:51.309 --> 00:02:55.299
democracy is desirable is that you have political competition.

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:57.560
I think that

00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:04.179
if you want to avoid winner-take-all politics, you really have to have competitive politics.

00:03:04.179 --> 00:03:07.499
Winner-take-all politics are really monopoly politics.

00:03:07.499 --> 00:03:08.989
And

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:13.049
monopolies are as destructive in politics as they are

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:14.350
in economics.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.699
We're not used to thinking that way.

00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:19.019
You know, when people say,

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:23.549
"Oh, isn't that great. There's bipartisan agreement on some bill."

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:28.299
Nobody stands up and says, "That's collusion and obstruction of democracy," right?

00:03:28.299 --> 00:03:32.649
Nobody stands up and says that, but that's really what it is, because

00:03:32.649 --> 00:03:36.239
what we've created in the US is a system where

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:37.569
the government

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:38.789
of the day

00:03:38.789 --> 00:03:41.779
can only really govern with the consent

00:03:41.779 --> 00:03:43.129
of the opposition.

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:44.769
RAE: Wait a minute, Ian,

00:03:44.769 --> 00:03:48.930
I don't think I quite follow the idea. 
SHAPIRO: Ok.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.589
RAE: That if the

00:03:51.589 --> 00:03:54.769
opposition or the various factions of the opposition

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.019
come together with the governing party and agree on something-- 
SHAPIRO: Yeah.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:01.099
RAE: --that that is

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:04.249
in any way contrary to the dictates of democracy.

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:09.669
SHAPIRO: No, if that's what happens. But if you design a system where the government cannot act UNLESS

00:04:09.669 --> 00:04:11.439
that happens.

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:16.769
RAE: It's a hell of a poor system that's solving big
problems.
SHAPIRO: I think that's right. So-- 

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:17.870
I will just

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.560
let Frederick come in in a second, just let me finish the thought. 

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.539
So, the thought here would be that--

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:27.800
why is competition good in democracy?

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.719
Well, you know,

00:04:29.719 --> 00:04:33.209
there are a lot of different reasons why competition is good.

00:04:33.209 --> 00:04:38.619
But one of the reasons is exactly analogous to why competition

00:04:38.619 --> 00:04:40.780
is good in the economy.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.870
Competition-- as a byproduct of competition, we get information, right?

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.350
We get information,

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.060
because the opposition

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.939
attacks the government, points out flaws in its arguments,

00:04:53.689 --> 00:04:58.369
shines light in dark corners, can undermine
corruption, and so on. Whereas if you

00:04:58.369 --> 00:04:59.090
essentially just

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.009
have to have a system to get everybody on board,

00:05:02.009 --> 00:05:07.270
you figure out what the distribution of bribes is to do that, and you do it, right?

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.429
So I think that

00:05:09.429 --> 00:05:13.080
the better system is the Westminster model where 

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.549
you have a strong government and you have an opposition which is not

00:05:17.549 --> 00:05:22.280
an ongoing monitor and participant in legislation,

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.599
but rather a government-in-waiting.

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:28.849
And that government-in-waiting,

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:31.270
with its shadow cabinet and all of that,

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.939
has the incentive

00:05:32.939 --> 00:05:36.360
to shine light in dark corners, to expose corruption,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.409
to-- just,

00:05:38.409 --> 00:05:43.689
you know, go watch Prime Minister's questions
online any day of the week, or read the British

00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:44.319
papers,

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:46.139
and you'll see

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:47.770
what genuine, more genuine

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.050
political competition

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.760
is like, than what we get here. So I think that

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.819
that's a more desirable

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:55.610
model. It's not perfect,

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.300
but as Churchill said, it's the worst
system compared to the others.

